{#
    Confirmation dialog — one instance per page, cloned out of this <template>
    by resources/js/confirm-dialog.js on the first confirmation.

    Why a component and not window.confirm(): the native dialog cannot carry a
    tone, cannot set an amount apart from the sentence around it, and cannot
    list the consequences of the action. Those are exactly the things an admin
    needs when releasing money or deleting a record, so the API takes all three
    (`tone`, `amount`, `bullets`) and this markup draws them.

    It is a <template> rather than hidden markup so the dialog's contents never
    exist in the accessibility tree or the tab order until something asks for
    them — a permanently-rendered confirm box is a focus trap waiting to be
    noticed by a screen reader.

    `role="alertdialog"` rather than `dialog`: the dialog interrupts the user
    mid-decision, which is what alertdialog describes. The two buttons are the
    only tab stops and the focus lands on the confirm action (see the JS),
    because a destructive action must never be one stray Enter away.
#}
<template id="th-confirm-template">
  <div class="th-confirm-backdrop" data-confirm-backdrop data-tone="danger" hidden>
    <div class="th-confirm"
         role="alertdialog"
         aria-modal="true"
         aria-labelledby="th-confirm-title"
         aria-describedby="th-confirm-message">

      <div class="th-confirm-icon" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg class="th-confirm-glyph" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
             stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
          {# The sprite is generated at build time (scripts/generate-sprite.php);
             `href` is rewritten per tone by the controller. #}
          <use data-confirm-glyph href="/assets/icons/lucide-sprite.svg#alert-triangle"></use>
        </svg>
      </div>

      <h2 class="th-confirm-title" id="th-confirm-title" data-confirm-title></h2>

      {# The figure the decision turns on, when there is one. #}
      <div class="th-confirm-amount" data-confirm-amount-box hidden>
        <span class="th-confirm-amount-value" data-confirm-amount></span>
        <span class="th-confirm-amount-label" data-confirm-amount-label></span>
      </div>

      <p class="th-confirm-message" id="th-confirm-message" data-confirm-message></p>

      {# What the action actually does, one consequence per line. #}
      <ul class="th-confirm-bullets" data-confirm-bullets hidden></ul>

      {# Cancel first in the DOM so it is the first tab stop; the confirm button
         takes focus on open (see resources/js/confirm-dialog.js). #}
      <div class="th-confirm-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost" data-confirm-cancel></button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-confirm" data-confirm-accept></button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>